  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nSurface high pressure continues to build across the Ohio Valley from \nthe northwest through Friday. Dry conditions and below average \ntemperatures persist with lows in the 50s and highs in the 70s to \nnear 80. \n\nThe surface high shifts east this weekend which will allow warmer \ntemperatures and higher dew points to move back into the area from \nthe south. Shower and thunderstorms chances return with the summer-\nlike conditions. Upper level shortwave troughs moving through the \nnorthwesterly flow aloft could lead to periods of greater storm \ncoverage at times. One potential period for more storm activity is \nMonday evening and Monday night. Instability may end up overlapping \nwith strong shear when a potential cluster of storms or MCS sinks \ninto the Ohio Valley from the northwest. Details remain very murky \nwithout CAM guidance to resolve potential convective details, but \nthere are some signs for convective activity somewhere in the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley late Monday.",
